<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert III of Artois</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1287">1287</a>–<a href="/years/1342">1342</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert III of Artois was a French noble, grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-ii-count-of-artois">Robert II, Count of Artois</a>, who spent decades claiming the county against his aunt Mahaut. When his use of forged documents was exposed, he was banished in 1332 and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, where he urged Edward III to claim the French throne. He was mortally wounded fighting for the English in Brittany in 1342.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-iii-of-artois.jpg" alt="Robert III of Artois" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_d%27artois.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Robert was the son of Philip of Artois, lord of Conches-en-Ouche, and Blanche, daughter of Duke John II of Brittany. His father died on 11 September 1298 of wounds received fighting the Flemish at the Battle of Furnes on 20 August 1297. When his grandfather, Count Robert II of Artois, was killed at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kortrijk">Courtrai</a> in 1302, the county passed by custom to Robert's aunt Mahaut, who stood closer to the dead count. Robert was then too young to contest the succession.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Robert played an important part in the accession of his brother-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-vi-of-france">Philip VI of France</a> and was for a time his trusted adviser. Through this influence he was granted the County of Beaumont-le-Roger in compensation for Artois. When Mahaut died in 1329 and Artois passed to her daughter, Countess Joan II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, Robert reopened the question of the succession, following the example of the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>.</p><p>In 1331 he was found guilty of forgery: his claim rested on thirty-four perjured depositions and a fake document made by Jeanne de Divion, who was burned at the stake. Robert ignored a fourth summons to appear before the king and on 8 April 1332 was sentenced in absentia to exile and the confiscation of his estates. He fled to his nephew, Marquis John II of Namur, and, when Philip asked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Liège to attack Namur, to his nephew-in-law Duke John III of Brabant. Philip compelled the duke to abandon Robert, who crossed the Channel to the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III of England</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In England Robert sat on Edward's royal council and gave the king detailed information about the French court. Later legends held that he urged Edward to claim the French crown, and many contemporary chroniclers traced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> to his influence: Philip VI named Edward's refusal to expel him as the reason for confiscating the Duchy of Guyenne in May 1337. Robert was briefly Earl of Richmond in 1341 and commanded the Anglo-Flemish army at the Battle of Saint-Omer in 1340.</p><p>Wounded during the retreat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-breton-succession">War of the Breton Succession</a>, he died of dysentery in November 1342. He was buried in Blackfriars Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>; his tomb is now in St Paul's Cathedral. In the late 1340s the poem Voeux du héron (Vow of the Heron) presented Edward's invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as the fulfilment of an oath sworn to Robert, and he is a leading character in Maurice Druon's novels Les Rois maudits.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Robert III of Artois” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Count Robert II was killed at Courtrai in 1302, and his daughter Mahaut defended her inheritance against her nephew <b>Robert III</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-artois">County of Artois</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1322" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Robert III of Artois"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4993 5724h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4993 5724h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1322">What was here in 1322? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
